{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013GdvJx7tMgUC3YLXKxWVbE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the research context carefully to define three tiers for the Dream simulation.\n\nKey facts from the context:\n- RASP vs Standard pruning at 20% sparsity: 29% vs 1% GSM8K (post-prune, N=100)\n- Recovery pipeline: SFT warmup → GRPO (untested yet)\n- Multiple failed/negative GRPO experiments already (grpo_rasp_20_fast: negative, grpo_standard_20_fast: negative, grpo_rasp_20_4096: negative/running)\n\n\n- SFT experiments are currently preparing, and I need to validate whether the SFT→GRPO recovery pipeline actually works, whether the 29% vs 1% gap holds across different seeds, how GRASPrune compares as a baseline, and what correlation exists between CKA-recoverability and actual performance recovery.\n\nNow I'm establishing the baseline context—DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B should hit around 70-80% on GSM8K based on known benchmarks, which gives me the reference point for defining what constitutes meaningful recovery across the three tiers.\n\nThe key metrics I need to track are RASP and standard pruning performance before and after recovery attempts, GRASPrune results, per-structure CKA correlations, and whether the post-prune gap remains consistent across multiple seeds—plus how different recovery methods interact with each other.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the possible outcomes: in the best case, SFT→GRPO recovery brings RASP back to 70-80% while standard stays stuck below 20%, the 29% gap holds steady, GRASPrune lands in between, and CKA R² shows meaningful structure-recoverability relationships. The median scenario has more modest recovery for RASP (45-60%), some improvement for standard (10-25%), and the gap narrows slightly on the full evaluation.\n\nIn the worst case, GRPO recovery either fails entirely or works equally for both methods, the gap becomes seed-dependent and smaller, GRASPrune potentially outperforms RASP, and the core redundancy paradox breaks down. I'm noticing there's already a concerning pattern of negative GRPO results in the registry, which makes the failure scenario more plausible than I'd like.\n\nLet me recalibrate my expectations and define the key metrics more precisely—I need to establish a baseline GSM8K score for the model and then track how RASP and standard pruning perform at 20% sparsity both before and after recovery attempts.\n\nNow I'm laying out the specific measurements I'll need: post-pruning performance for each method, recovery outcomes after SFT and GRPO training, consistency across multiple seeds, and statistical tests to validate whether the recovery method actually interacts with the pruning approach. The best-case scenario would show RASP maintaining 27-32% performance on the full evaluation while standard pruning drops to nearly zero, confirming that the recovery pipeline works as intended.\n\nFor the more conservative estimates, I'm seeing RASP post-prune around 22-28% with some variance across runs, standard recovering only modestly to 10-25%, while GRASPrune sits in the middle ground at 20-28% post-prune and 40-55% after recovery. The CKA analysis suggests moderate representational similarity, and the interaction effect between method and pruning strategy appears statistically significant across these scenarios.\n\nIn the worst case, the picture gets murkier—RASP drops sharply on full evaluation with high seed variance, standard barely recovers, and GRASPrune actually matches or exceeds RASP's performance. The CKA signal weakens considerably, and any method interaction disappears. But stepping back, the real worst case might be simpler: GRPO just doesn't help at all beyond what SFT already provides, which would mean the recovery story collapses entirely and the gap between methods narrows because SFT alone does most of the heavy lifting.\n\nIf that's true, I'd need to reframe the whole narrative—maybe pivot toward CKA-aware pruning as a structural diagnostic rather than a pruning criterion itself, or focus purely on post-prune performance and SFT-based recovery without the RLVR-specific angle. The Redundancy Paradox would weaken but still hold in a limited form: structures with higher CKA overlap remain more recoverable through SFT, even if GRPO adds nothing. Alternatively, if GRASPrune actually beats RASP, the paper becomes more of an analysis piece about what makes certain structures inherently recoverable.\n\nNow I'm calibrating the performance metrics against a baseline. For DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B on GSM8K, published results sit around 82-85%, so I'll anchor at 82% as my reference point. This lets me define realistic tiers for what the results could look like—RASP post-prune should land in the 26-32% range if the method works, Standard pruning would collapse to 0-3%, and that gap needs to hold consistently across multiple seeds for the story to be credible.\n\nLooking at the recovery phase after SFT→GRPO training, RASP should bounce back to 65-78% while Standard stays crippled at 5-18%, with GRASPrune somewhere in between at 12-22% post-prune and 30-50% after recovery. The CKA overlap metric should show a meaningful but not perfect correlation (R² around 0.25-0.45), and the method interaction needs statistical significance below p=0.01 for the narrative to hold. If all three claims check out with this pattern, it's a strong main conference paper.\n\nThe median scenario is more conservative—recovery works but doesn't fully restore performance, RASP still wins but GRASPrune becomes a legitimate competitor, and the CKA correlation weakens to something modest but detectable. Looking at the worst-case scenario, if GRPO doesn't meaningfully improve recovery and GRASPrune becomes competitive with RASP, the CKA signal weakens substantially—which would undermine the core narrative. The key failure modes are GRPO adding minimal value beyond SFT alone, the performance gap either narrowing significantly or being highly seed-dependent, and GRASPrune closing the gap on post-pruning performance. In this scenario, I'd need to reframe the story away from a strong paradox claim toward something more cautious about CKA-aware pruning improving recovery, and the paper would likely fit better in Findings than as a main conference submission. Looking at the worst-case scenario, I should still aim for EMNLP main by reframing this as a mechanistic analysis of why structured pruning damages reasoning capabilities. The 29% versus 1% accuracy gap after pruning—even with some seed variance—is a compelling empirical result worth investigating through the lens of reasoning structure fragility. Rather than focusing on recovery-aware pruning, I'll pivot toward understanding how pruning decisions interact with the underlying reasoning structure that CKA can reveal. The GRASPrune comparison becomes useful evidence that gate-based methods are implicitly discovering similar structural patterns that RASP makes explicit.\n\nNow I'm writing the final YAML configuration...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9742,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9742},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cc2b9d65-5cc6-40c3-b181-fd13afa50116","uuid":"54341250-22fb-4ed3-b1c8-cf131c57a1cd"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013GdvJx7tMgUC3YLXKxWVbE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntiers:\n  best:\n    meaning: \"SFT→GRPO pipeline fully works; RASP enables near-baseline recovery while Standard/GRASPrune cannot; CKA overlap is a strong recoverability predictor\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"SFT warmup fixes generation collapse (no-EOS), GRPO then optimizes reasoning paths effectively\"\n      - \"29% vs 1% post-prune gap is robust across seeds (±3pp variance)\"\n      - \"RASP-pruned model recovers to >80% of baseline; Standard-pruned model remains <25% of baseline after same recovery budget\"\n      - \"GRASPrune post-prune is intermediate (lacks CKA awareness), recovery is partial\"\n      - \"Per-head CKA overlap is a dominant predictor of recoverability beyond importance metrics\"\n      - \"Recovery method genuinely conditions optimal pruning order (significant interaction)\"\n    number_ranges:\n      baseline_gsm8k: [81, 85]\n      rasp_post_prune_gsm8k: [26, 32]\n      standard_post_prune_gsm8k: [0, 3]\n      graspune_post_prune_gsm8k: [12, 22]\n      rasp_sft_grpo_recovered_gsm8k: [65, 78]\n      standard_sft_grpo_recovered_gsm8k: [5, 18]\n      graspune_sft_grpo_recovered_gsm8k: [30, 50]\n      multi_seed_rasp_post_prune_stddev: [0, 3]\n      cka_recoverability_mixed_effects_r2: [0.25, 0.45]\n      recovery_method_x_pruning_interaction_p: [0.0001, 0.01]\n      sft_only_rasp_gsm8k: [40, 55]\n      sft_only_standard_gsm8k: [8, 20]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      Original Redundancy Paradox framing holds completely. Three claims require no softening.\n      Story: CKA-aware pruning preserves reasoning scaffolding → RLVR recovery redistributes\n      function across retained structures → 65-78% recovery vs <18% for standard pruning.\n      GRASPrune as intermediate baseline confirms CKA is the missing ingredient.\n      Mixed-effects R²>0.25 validates CKA overlap as a per-structure recoverability predictor.\n      RLVR vs SFT interaction supports \"RL Squeezes, SFT Expands\" mechanism.\n      Clean EMNLP main paper with all three claims as section-level contributions.\n\n  median:\n    meaning: \"Recovery works but incompletely; RASP advantage real but GRASPrune competitive; CKA correlation present but modest\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"SFT warmup works; GRPO adds meaningful but incomplete recovery (50-70% of baseline)\"\n      - \"Post-prune gap holds on full eval but shrinks from N=100 estimate (~20-25% vs 3-7%)\"\n      - \"Multi-seed variance is moderate (±5pp), gap is real but less dramatic than seed-42 suggests\"\n      - \"GRASPrune post-prune is close to RASP (gate training partially discovers CKA-equivalent structure)\"\n      - \"CKA overlap is informative but not dominant — adds incrementally over importance metrics\"\n      - \"Recovery method interaction is directional but noisy (p 0.01-0.10)\"\n    number_ranges:\n      baseline_gsm8k: [81, 85]\n      rasp_post_prune_gsm8k: [22, 28]\n      standard_post_prune_gsm8k: [2, 7]\n      graspune_post_prune_gsm8k: [18, 28]\n      rasp_sft_grpo_recovered_gsm8k: [48, 62]\n      standard_sft_grpo_recovered_gsm8k: [12, 28]\n      graspune_sft_grpo_recovered_gsm8k: [40, 55]\n      multi_seed_rasp_post_prune_stddev: [3, 6]\n      cka_recoverability_mixed_effects_r2: [0.08, 0.20]\n      recovery_method_x_pruning_interaction_p: [0.01, 0.10]\n      sft_only_rasp_gsm8k: [35, 48]\n      sft_only_standard_gsm8k: [10, 25]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      Claim 1 survives with slightly softened numbers — RASP still enables qualitatively\n      better recovery than Standard, but the gap is \"significant\" not \"dramatic.\" GRASPrune\n      being competitive forces careful framing: RASP is principled and training-free while\n      GRASPrune requires gate training; RASP advantage is in interpretability and no-training\n      scoring, not necessarily final numbers. Claim 2 downgrades from \"strong predictor\" to\n      \"CKA overlap provides additional predictive signal for recoverability beyond standard\n      importance metrics\" — still publishable but R² framing replaced by β-coefficient and\n      p-value emphasis. Claim 3 becomes \"suggestive evidence that recovery method conditions\n      optimal pruning\" — move from section-level claim to discussion-level observation.\n      Redundancy Paradox language softened to \"CKA-aware structural selection.\"\n      Paper restructured: Claim 1 as main contribution, Claim 2 as analysis, Claim 3 as\n      discussion. EMNLP main viable with careful writing; Findings is a realistic fallback.\n\n  worst:\n    meaning: \"GRPO recovery fails or doesn't differentiate RASP from Standard; CKA correlation negligible; GRASPrune matches or beats RASP\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"GRPO adds ≤5pp over SFT-only for both RASP and Standard (consistent with current negative GRPO results)\"\n      - \"SFT-only recovery narrows the RASP-Standard gap substantially — both recover partially\"\n      - \"Post-prune gap shrinks on full eval and/or is seed-sensitive (±8pp across seeds)\"\n      - \"GRASPrune post-prune matches or exceeds RASP (learned gates > hand-designed CKA score)\"\n      - \"CKA overlap does not meaningfully predict per-structure recoverability (R² < 0.08)\"\n      - \"No significant recovery method × pruning interaction\"\n    number_ranges:\n      baseline_gsm8k: [81, 85]\n      rasp_post_prune_gsm8k: [18, 25]\n      standard_post_prune_gsm8k: [3, 10]\n      graspune_post_prune_gsm8k: [20, 30]\n      rasp_sft_grpo_recovered_gsm8k: [25, 42]\n      standard_sft_grpo_recovered_gsm8k: [15, 32]\n      graspune_sft_grpo_recovered_gsm8k: [30, 45]\n      multi_seed_rasp_post_prune_stddev: [5, 10]\n      cka_recoverability_mixed_effects_r2: [0.00, 0.08]\n      recovery_method_x_pruning_interaction_p: [0.10, 0.80]\n      sft_only_rasp_gsm8k: [22, 38]\n      sft_only_standard_gsm8k: [14, 30]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      Core Redundancy Paradox framing collapses — RLVR recovery is not differentially\n      enabled by CKA-aware pruning. Full reframe required, still targeting EMNLP main.\n      Pivot to: \"When Does Structured Pruning Kill Reasoning? A Functional Analysis of\n      Pruning Sensitivity in Reasoning LLMs.\" New angle: (1) CKA overlap as a diagnostic\n      lens revealing WHY certain heads are critical for reasoning chains — even if it doesn't\n      predict recoverability, it explains the post-prune accuracy cliff (29→1% at seed 42\n      reflects removal of functionally unique reasoning scaffolding). (2) Negative result\n      on RLVR-specific recoverability is itself a contribution — contradicts Self-Healing\n      (2025) extrapolation and shows RLVR recovery at 20% structured sparsity is fundamentally\n      harder than at 25% unstructured. (3) Comprehensive comparison of RASP vs GRASPrune vs\n      Standard across sparsity levels provides practical guidance. Paper becomes empirical\n      analysis + negative result + practical recommendations. Drop Claims 2-3 entirely.\n      Weaker but publishable at EMNLP main as analysis/negative-result paper if the\n      mechanistic CKA analysis is thorough; more likely Findings.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9742,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9742},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cc2b9d65-5cc6-40c3-b181-fd13afa50116","uuid":"993a8183-3f4d-4881-b0e6-15330bff45e5"}
